---
/**
 * SPEC §8: every derived metric, its formula, its source, its known limitations
 * and the update cadence. Shipped with the milestone that introduces the
 * metrics, not later.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import meta from "../data/meta.json";
import screener from "../data/screener.json";

const growthFloor = meta.thresholds.growth_floor_usd;
const maxStaleRuns = meta.thresholds.max_stale_runs;
const universeSize = meta.universe_size;

// Counted rather than written down, so the regulatory-coverage argument below
// cannot drift as the universe grows — the failure this page was just fixed for.
const NO_APPROVALS = new Set([
  "payer",
  "provider",
  "distributor",
  "hcit",
  "clinical_stage_biotech",
]);
const DEVICE_ROUTE = new Set(["medtech", "diagnostics", "life_science_tools"]);

const bySubsector = screener.companies.map((c) => c.subsector);
const noApprovals = bySubsector.filter((s) => NO_APPROVALS.has(s)).length;
const deviceRoute = bySubsector.filter((s) => DEVICE_ROUTE.has(s)).length;
const drugRoute = universeSize - noApprovals - deviceRoute;
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <p class="muted">
    <a href="/screener/">Screener</a> · <a href="/companies/">Company pages</a> ·
    <a href="/subsectors/">Subsectors</a> · <a href="/about/">About</a>
  </p>

  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p>
    Every figure on this site comes from one of two primary sources: SEC EDGAR XBRL company facts
    for financials, and ClinicalTrials.gov for the pipeline. Nothing is estimated, smoothed or
    adjusted. Where a figure cannot be established honestly it is reported as
    <strong>null</strong>, which is not zero, or as <strong>n/m</strong>, which means the
    arithmetic is valid but the result describes nothing useful.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    Data as of {meta.generated_at} · pipeline version {meta.pipeline_version} · refreshed weekly.
  </p>

  <h2>Update cadence</h2>
  <p>
    The pipeline re-fetches the whole universe weekly and commits the result, which rebuilds the
    site. Fundamentals move quarterly, so weekly is generous; the registry data moves continuously,
    and its as-of date is recorded separately on every company page.
  </p>
  <p>
    A refresh that fails for one company does not hold up the rest. That company keeps its previous
    figures and is marked — on the screener with a dagger beside the ticker, and on its own page
    with a banner and the date the figures actually come from.
    The fallback is bounded at {maxStaleRuns} consecutive failed refreshes, after which the company is removed from the site
    rather than carried further. Figures that are a month old while presented under a current
    as-of date are a worse failure than an admitted gap, and every guard on this page exists for
    the same reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Deriving quarters</h2>
  <p>
    Income-statement and cash-flow facts are frequently filed year to date rather than as discrete
    quarters, and a fourth quarter is almost never filed on its own. Quarters are obtained by
    differencing successive year-to-date figures within a single XBRL tag, and Q4 by subtracting
    the nine-month figure from the annual. Three rules govern this:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Tags are resolved per period, not per company.</strong> Filers migrate tags mid-life,
      so a single choice for a whole company either loses recent data or loses history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Arithmetic never crosses tags.</strong> A quarter is derived inside one tag's facts;
      only finished quarters are compared. Subtracting a figure on one basis from a figure on
      another produces a plausible, wrong number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restatements resolve to the most recently filed value</strong>, on both sides of every
      subtraction, so a derived quarter never mixes vintages.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Metrics</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Revenue TTM</td>
        <td>Sum of the four most recent discrete quarters</td>
        <td>Null unless four quarters tile the window exactly.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Revenue growth YoY</td>
        <td>(TTM − prior TTM) ÷ |prior TTM|</td>
        <td>
          <strong>n/m where the prior trailing year was below {growthFloor} USD.</strong> A
          percentage off a near-zero base describes the base, not the business. n/m sorts to the
          end of the column in both directions, so these names cannot top a growth screen.
          Suppressed entirely where the window spans a restated fiscal year.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Operating margin</td>
        <td>Operating income TTM ÷ revenue TTM</td>
        <td>
          Both sides must cover the same twelve months. Several large-cap filers present no
          operating income subtotal at all — see below.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>OCF margin</td>
        <td>Operating cash flow TTM ÷ revenue TTM</td>
        <td>Same window requirement.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>R&D intensity</td>
        <td>R&D TTM ÷ revenue TTM</td>
        <td>
          For pre-revenue companies the denominator is operating expenses instead, and the value is
          flagged. The two denominators are not comparable with each other.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Net cash</td>
        <td>cash + short-term investments − total debt</td>
        <td>
          Negative for leveraged large caps, which is expected. Where a filer tags no borrowings,
          debt is treated as zero and the value is flagged — a debt-free company and one that
          failed to tag its debt are indistinguishable from the filings.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Net margin</td>
        <td>Net income TTM ÷ revenue TTM</td>
        <td><code>NetIncomeLoss</code> is universally tagged, which is why this and OCF margin carry the headline.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Cash runway</td>
        <td>(cash + short-term investments) ÷ mean(|quarterly OCF|) over the trailing four</td>
        <td>
          Null where trailing operating cash flow is positive — that is the absence of a runway,
          not a short one. Suppressed where the cash figure includes restricted cash that the filer
          does not tag separately.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Clinical pipeline metrics</h2>
  <p>
    Counted from ClinicalTrials.gov, restricted to <strong>interventional</strong> studies where the
    company is the <strong>lead</strong> sponsor. Both restrictions matter: including observational
    work counts investigator-initiated studies the company did not run, and including collaborator
    roles counts other people's trials.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sponsor names are matched whole-field against an explicit list per company, never by free text.
    Subsidiaries register under their own names — Pfizer's appear as sentences such as
    <code>Wyeth is now a wholly owned subsidiary of Pfizer</code> — so the list is built by an
    enumeration tool and confirmed by hand. Fuzzy matching here produces silent, plausible-looking
    misattribution.
  </p>
  <p>
    A trial declaring several phases counts once, at the highest phase it reaches. "Active" means
    recruiting, active but not recruiting, enrolling by invitation, or not yet recruiting.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Pipeline depth score</td>
        <td>1×Phase 1 + 3×Phase 2 + 8×Phase 3 + 12×Phase 4, active trials only</td>
        <td>
          A transparent heuristic, not a proprietary model. The weights rise steeply because a
          Phase 3 represents far more committed capital than a Phase 1. <strong>Null for medtech
          and diagnostics</strong>: phases are a drug-development construct, and device studies are
          pivotal, feasibility or post-market instead. A zero there would say "no pipeline", which
          is false; null says "not applicable", which is true. No parallel medtech heuristic is
          offered, because inventing one would put a different metric under the same heading.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Pipeline concentration</td>
        <td>Herfindahl index across the conditions of active trials</td>
        <td>
          1.0 means every active trial addresses one condition — single-asset risk. A trial listing
          several conditions contributes a fraction to each, so one broad trial is not counted as
          several narrow ones. Null below three active trials: the counts stay visible, but any
          figure distributed across one or two of them describes the denominator, not the company.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Clinical momentum</td>
        <td>Trials started in the trailing 12 months − the prior 12 months</td>
        <td>
          Registration lags the start date, so recent months are systematically under-counted. A
          small negative reading is not evidence of contraction.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Discontinuation rate</td>
        <td>(terminated + withdrawn) ÷ (completed + terminated + withdrawn), all time</td>
        <td>
          Suppressed below ten resolved trials. On a handful, one termination moves the rate by
          tens of percentage points and describes the sample rather than the company.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>R&D per late-stage programme</td>
        <td>R&D TTM ÷ count of active Phase 2 and 3 trials</td>
        <td>
          <strong>Shown on company pages only, not in the screener.</strong> Trial count is a weak
          proxy for programme count: one Phase 3 run across four indications registers as four
          trials, while a basket trial across the same four registers as one. The denominator
          therefore tracks registry practice rather than how R&D is actually allocated, which makes
          the metric defensible within a subsector and not across one — so it is kept off a screen
          people would rank on. It also spans both data sources: the R&D figure ends on a fiscal
          quarter end while the trial count is whatever the registry said at fetch time, so the two
          were never true at the same instant. Where that gap exceeds a quarter the value is flagged.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>

  <h3>The clinical perimeter matches the financial reporting perimeter</h3>
  <p>
    Where a business has been divested, its trials are excluded even for the years before the
    divestiture. Merck's FY2020 financials are already restated for the Organon spin-off, so
    counting pre-spin Organon trials would put the clinical and financial histories on different
    corporate perimeters, and every metric spanning both — R&D per late-stage programme above all —
    would compare a broad trial base against a narrow revenue base.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two exclusions follow from this: Organon from Merck, and the Upjohn business that became
    Viatris from Pfizer. Pre-divestiture clinical history shrinks as a result. That is the intended
    trade-off: a truncated series that matches the financials beats a complete one that does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Acquisitions need no such treatment, because ClinicalTrials.gov rewrites sponsor records
    retroactively. Immunomedics' trials, including those started in 2012, now carry
    <code>Gilead Sciences</code> as lead sponsor — matching how the financials consolidate them.
  </p>

  <h3>Two companies are called Merck</h3>
  <p>
    Merck &amp; Co (MRK, this universe) and Merck KGaA of Darmstadt are unrelated listed companies
    sharing a name; outside North America the branding is reversed. A query on the bare word
    returns both. Merck KGaA's strings — including
    <code>SpringWorks Therapeutics, Inc., a healthcare company of Merck KGaA</code>, which reads
    exactly like a Merck &amp; Co subsidiary — are on an explicit exclusion list, and a test fails
    the build if any of their trials ever enters Merck's record.
  </p>

  <h3>Operating income is not universally reported</h3>
  <p>
    Large pharmaceutical companies often present no operating income subtotal, running from expense
    lines straight to pre-tax earnings. Pfizer, Merck and Johnson &amp; Johnson all tag none.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not reconstructed. Two approaches were tried and both failed for the same structural
    reason: the composition of the non-operating block is filer-specific in XBRL, so there is no
    stable identity to invert. Back-tested against filers that do report the subtotal, the nearest
    available substitute was wrong by 1.8 to 12.6 percentage points of margin — plausible-looking
    figures, wrong by enough to reorder a ranking. Operating margin is therefore reported where
    tagged and null where not, and net margin and OCF margin carry the headline instead. For this
    universe OCF margin is arguably the better measure anyway: cash-based, and harder to flatter
    with amortisation and one-off charges.
  </p>

  <h3>Cash sometimes includes restricted balances</h3>
  <p>
    The balance-sheet cash tag excludes restricted cash; the cash-flow statement's reconciling
    figure includes it. Where a filer abandons the former, the latter is used and flagged. For a
    cash position the overstatement is minor. For cash runway it is a conceptual error, because
    restricted cash cannot fund operations, so the restricted portion is netted off where the filer
    tags it separately and the metric is suppressed where it does not.
  </p>

  <h3>Two measurement bases for R&D</h3>
  <p>
    Some filers report R&D including acquired in-process R&D and some excluding it, and a few
    switch partway through their history. Acquired IPR&D charges are lumpy and deal-driven, so the
    two are not comparable. The basis is recorded on every value and the switchover point is
    identified, but the figures are never normalised to a common basis.
  </p>

  <h3>IFRS filers are excluded</h3>
  <p>
    GSK, AstraZeneca, Sanofi and BioNTech report under IFRS. Under IAS 38 qualifying development
    costs are capitalised and amortised, while US GAAP expenses essentially all R&D as incurred, so
    R&D intensity and cash runway measure different things on the two bases. Several also file
    annually on 20-F rather than quarterly, so the discrete quarterly series this site is built on
    does not exist for them.
  </p>

  <h3>Restated years suppress comparisons</h3>
  <p>
    Where an annual figure was restated but its quarters were not — typically after a spin-off —
    the quarters are still reported as filed, but any growth or comparison metric spanning that
    year returns null. A comparison across such a year measures the restatement rather than the
    business.
  </p>

  <h3>Regulatory and reimbursement data are not covered</h3>
  <p>
    Every company record carries a <code>regulatory</code> field and it is null. openFDA would
    populate it with approval counts and dates. It has not been built, because the coverage does
    not justify the matching risk.
  </p>
  <p>
    openFDA identifies companies by sponsor and applicant strings that match neither tickers nor
    SEC registrant names — the FDA knows Pfizer as <code>PFIZER</code> and Bristol-Myers Squibb
    under four strings beginning <code>BMS</code>, none containing "Bristol" or "Squibb". Covering
    it means a third hand-curated mapping of exactly the kind described above, where a wrong entry
    misattributes one company's record to another and looks entirely plausible while doing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What that buys is narrower than it appears.
    Of the {universeSize} companies here, <strong>{noApprovals}</strong> have no approvals to count at all —
    payers, providers, distributors, health IT and the clinical-stage names.
    The other {deviceRoute + drugRoute} split across two datasets that are not the same measurement
    and do not belong in one column: {drugRoute} go through drug approvals, and {deviceRoute} through device clearances. For the
    vaccines and infectious-disease companies this universe is weighted toward, approval counts are
    in any case dominated by supplemental and label-expansion filings rather than new products, so
    the number would read as a measure of output while mostly measuring paperwork.
  </p>
  <p>
    Medicare Part B and Part D spending was assessed as an alternative and rejected for the same
    reason. It is the closest public proxy for product-level US revenue, which segment reporting
    does not give. But Part B carries no manufacturer field at all; Part D truncates manufacturer
    names to fifteen characters, so the matching key is lossy before any curation begins; and
    vaccines are 0.3% of Part D rows, which is to say the dataset is emptiest exactly where this
    site is deepest. On a September 2026 assessment of the live data, around eighteen companies
    here would have gained anything at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both remain possible later. The <code>regulatory</code> field is kept null rather than removed
    so that the shape of the data would not change if either arrives.
  </p>
</Base>
